------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Presentations of groups
--
-- Structurally equivalent circuits have the same path-sum, up to
-- renaming the path variables (Amy, QPL 2018, remark 2.8)
--
-- Remark 2.8: "circuits which are equivalent up to symmetric monoidal
-- laws are strictly equal in the path-sum picture."  For circuits
-- over {H, CNOT, R_k, R_k†} written as lists of gates on n wires
-- (PathSum.CRK.Circuit), the laws that can relate two such lists are
-- the interchange law -- two gates on disjoint wires may be applied in
-- either order -- and the symmetry, which here is a relabelling of the
-- wires; associativity and the unit laws are invisible in a list of
-- gates on a fixed set of wires.
--
-- Structural equivalence C ∼ C′ is the congruence on gate lists
-- generated by exchanging adjacent gates on disjoint wires
-- (Disjoint: no wire of one is a wire of the other): the reflexive,
-- symmetric, transitive closure of the exchange, closed under adding a
-- gate in front (∼-cons) and hence under concatenation on either side
-- (∼-++).  Circuits on disjoint wires commute as blocks
-- (∼-parallel), which is bifunctoriality, the first example of the
-- remark, read on circuits.
--
-- The theorem (structural): if C ∼ C′ then ⟦ C ⟧ ≡ᴿ⟨ π ⟩ ⟦ C′ ⟧ for a
-- bijection π of the path variables read off the derivation -- the
-- same normalisation, and the coefficients of the phase and of every
-- output of ⟦ C′ ⟧ equal, as integers, to those of ⟦ C ⟧ once its
-- path variables are renamed by π (PathSum.Permute).  A fortiori the
-- phases are congruent modulo 2^M and the outputs modulo 2 after the
-- renaming (structural-≈, PathSum.Permute.Sound's ≈ᴿ), and
-- ⟦ C ⟧ ≋ ⟦ C′ ⟧ (structural-≋).  The renaming is the only
-- difference, and it is needed: a Hadamard allocates its path
-- variable at the head, so exchanging two Hadamards exchanges two
-- path variables; exchanging any other pair renames nothing.
--
-- The symmetry: relabelling the wires of a circuit along a bijection
-- σ (relabelC, PathSum.CRK.Trace's mapC) relabels its path-sum
-- (relabel-⟦⟧): ⟦ relabelC σ C ⟧ is ⟦ C ⟧ with input x_i renamed
-- x_(σ i) and the output of wire i moved to wire σ i, coefficient by
-- coefficient, the path variables only re-indexed along
-- paths C ≡ paths (relabelC σ C).  So C ≃⟨ σ ⟩ C′ -- C′ structurally
-- equivalent to C relabelled -- gives relabel σ ⟦ C ⟧ ≡ᴿ⟨ π ⟩ ⟦ C′ ⟧
-- (structural-≃), and so relabel σ ⟦ C ⟧ ≋ ⟦ C′ ⟧: ⟦ C′ ⟧ is ⟦ C ⟧
-- conjugated by the permutation of the wires
-- (PathSum.Permute.Sound.amp-relabel).
--
-- How the proof goes.  The path-sum of a circuit is read path by path
-- through PathSum.CRK.Trace: along a path, given as a stream s of
-- bits, the phase and the outputs are a configuration that the gates
-- update one at a time, the gate g of g ∷ C reading the bit
-- s (norm C).  Two gates on disjoint wires update the configuration in
-- either order alike (gate-comm).  So a derivation of C ∼ C′ gives a
-- bijection ρ of the bit positions, fixing every position from norm C
-- on (a Reorder), with trace C′ s ≈ trace C (s ∘ ρ) (reorders): the
-- exchange of two Hadamards is the transposition of the two positions
-- they read, the other exchanges the identity.  The trace reads only
-- the first norm C bits (trace-below), so ρ restricts to a bijection
-- of the path variables of ⟦ C ⟧ and ⟦ C′ ⟧ (toPerm), along which
-- their phases take the same values and their outputs the same bits;
-- by Möbius uniqueness (PathSum.Permute.≡ᴿ-by-values) the
-- coefficients agree.  No polynomial is computed or compared.
--
-- How far "strictly equal" goes.  The coefficients agree exactly --
-- the phases as integers, not only modulo 2^M, and the outputs as
-- integers, not only modulo 2 -- but (1) only after renaming the
-- path variables, the bound variables of the sum, which is
-- α-conversion, and the renaming cannot be dropped: H on wire 0 then
-- H on wire 1 against the other order (HH-∼) have phases differing at
-- the coefficient of x₀ y₀ (renaming-needed); (2) coefficient by
-- coefficient, since polynomials are
-- functions and --safe has no function extensionality; (3) with the
-- normalisations and the numbers of path variables equal as numbers,
-- not definitionally (norm-∼, and π itself).
------------------------------------------------------------------------

